entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. The bounce is on in `serve` (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) and applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): nothing is pushed and no PR is opened, and close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human`, with one comment that lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The same applies when the bounce is off. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies; a test drives the whole path (bounce, re-run, gate, approval, worker, reviewers and the three negative cases) against fake agents with no network.\n373:**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. The merger can request a conflict round through the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound` with reason `conflict`): a computed conflict at a head with no round yet records a `requested` event and the worker merges `main` into the branch. A conflict round lands when the branch head is a merge commit that includes the round's head (a merge-only resolution changes no code against it, so the no-code check does not apply); otherwise it caps. Conflict and review rounds share `max_review_rounds`, and each event records its reason. `servecmd` wires the review fix loop into the merger as `Rounds`, so a conflict starts a conflict round. In it the controller fetches `main` itself, trial-merges it into a scratch worktree (no commit, attributes from the empty tree, no rerere, renormalize or `-X`), and gives the worker an untrusted `conflict.md` (capped, escaped paths; no hunks). The first agent step starts with that merge in its worktree. After the step the controller runs no git in the agent's worktree: it reads the recorded conflicted files for leftover markers (advisory), measures the PR's own diff against the fetched `main` tip, not the job's base, and hands the committer the full diff against `main` with `MergeParent` set to that tip. The code lane does not run `git merge`: it fetches `main` into a private ref and refuses unless it equals `MergeParent` (`ErrMergeParent`), requires the branch still at the job's base (`ErrConflict`, no retry), writes the declared files over main's tree, and checks that the result differs from `main` only in declared paths that pass `CheckCodePath`, so `.github/`, `.ard/`, `.gitmodules` and `.gitattributes` equal `main` byte for byte. It commits with parents `[branch tip, main tip]` and pushes with a lease on the branch tip (a merge whose tree equals main's is still committed). A GitHub rejection of a merge that brings in workflow changes ends `needs-human`. The loop counts a conflict round as landed only when the head is a merge that includes the round's head and whose second parent is on `main`. The conflict-round section of the worker prompt is a separate human PR.\n421:- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and, with the merger's `Rounds` dependency wired in `servecmd` (#255), it becomes a conflict round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\nbranch.go\ncandidates.go\ncandidates_test.go\nconflict.go\nconflict_round_test.go\nconflict_test.go\ndoc.go\nfacts.go\nfeedback.go\nguard.go\nharness_test.go\nloop.go\nloop_test.go\npr.go\nprretry.go\nrun.go\ntype.go\nunit_test.go\ninternal/worker/conflict_round_test.go:92:func TestConflictRoundCleanMerge(t *testing.T) {","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"511333aa-7a4d-4af6-889b-5d4ff439c634","uuid":"a969e3a0-e9a1-4fb8-8a95-19675312233d","timestamp":"2026-11-27T20:15:18.662Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"package worker\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strconv\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/agentjob\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/executor\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/interna